== Faith, What Is It?

#smallcaps[What] is this faith of which it is said, “By grace you#super[+] have been saved, #emph[through faith]”? There are many descriptions of faith; but almost all the definitions I have come across have made me understand it less than I did before I saw them. The negro said, when he read the chapter, that he would #emph[confound] it; and very likely he did, though he meant to #emph[expound] it. We may explain faith until nobody understands it. I hope I will not be guilty of that fault. Faith is the simplest of all things, and perhaps because of its simplicity it is all the harder to explain.

What is faith? #emph[It is made up of three things — Knowledge, belief, and trust. Knowledge] comes first. “How can they believe in the One of whom they have not heard?” I need to be told a fact before I can possibly believe it. “Faith comes by hearing”; we must first hear, so that we may know what is to be believed. “Those who know Your name trust in You.” A measure of knowledge is essential to faith; that is why it is important to gain knowledge. “Incline your ear and come to Me; listen, so that your soul may live”: that was the word of the prophet of old, and it is still the word of the gospel. Search the Scriptures and learn what the Holy Spirit teaches about Christ and his salvation. Seek to know God: “For anyone who approaches Him must believe that He exists and that He rewards those who earnestly seek Him.” May the Holy Spirit give you the spirit of knowledge, and of the fear of the Lord! Know the gospel: know what the good news is, how it speaks of free forgiveness, and of a change of heart, of adoption into the family of God, and of countless other blessings. Know especially Christ Jesus the Son of God, the Savior of men, united to us by his human nature, and yet one with God, and so able to act as mediator between God and man, able to lay his hand on both, and to be the connecting link between the sinner and the Judge of all the earth. Strive to know more and more of Christ Jesus. Strive especially to know the doctrine of the sacrifice of Christ; for the point on which saving faith chiefly fixes itself is this — “God was reconciling the world to Himself in Christ, not imputing men’s trespasses to them.” Know that Jesus was “made a curse for us, as it is written: ‘Cursed is everyone who is hung on a tree.’” Drink deeply of the doctrine of the substitutionary work of Christ; for in it lies the sweetest possible comfort for the guilty sons of men, since the Lord “made Him to be sin on our behalf, so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God.” Faith begins with knowledge.

The mind goes on to #emph[believe] that these things are true. The soul believes that God exists, and that he hears the cries of sincere hearts; that the gospel is from God; that justification by faith is the great truth which God has revealed in these last days by his Spirit more clearly than before. Then the heart believes that Jesus is really and truly our God and Savior, the Redeemer of men, the Prophet, Priest, and King of his people. All this is accepted as sure truth, not to be questioned. I pray that you may come to this at once. Come to believe firmly that “the blood of Jesus Christ, God’s dear Son, cleanses us from all sin”; that his sacrifice is complete and fully accepted by God on man’s behalf, so that he who believes in Jesus is not condemned. Believe these truths as you believe any other statements; for the difference between common faith and saving faith lies mainly in the things it is exercised on. Believe the witness of God just as you believe the testimony of your own father or friend. “If we accept the testimony of men, the testimony of God is greater.”

So far you have advanced toward faith; only one more ingredient is needed to complete it, and that is #emph[trust]. Commit yourself to the merciful God; rest your hope on the gracious gospel; entrust your soul to the dying and living Savior; wash away your sins in the atoning blood; accept his perfect righteousness, and all is well. Trust is the lifeblood of faith; there is no saving faith without it. The Puritans used to explain faith by the word “recumbency.” It means leaning on something. Lean with all your weight on Christ. It would be a better illustration still if I said: fall at full length, and lie on the Rock of Ages. Cast yourself on Jesus; rest in him; commit yourself to him. Once that is done, you have exercised saving faith. Faith is not a blind thing; for faith begins with knowledge. It is not a speculative thing; for faith believes facts of which it is sure. It is not an impractical, dreamy thing; for faith trusts, and stakes its destiny on the truth of revelation. That is one way of describing what faith is; I wonder whether I have “confounded” it already.

Let me try again. #emph[Faith is believing that Christ is what he is said to be, and that he will do what he has promised to do, and then expecting this of him]. The Scriptures speak of Jesus Christ as being God, God in human flesh; as being perfect in his character; as being made a sin offering on our behalf; as bearing our sins in his own body on the tree. The Scripture speaks of him as having finished transgression, made an end of sin, and brought in everlasting righteousness. The sacred records also tell us that he “rose again from the dead,” that he “always lives to intercede for us,” that he has gone up into glory and taken possession of heaven on behalf of his people, and that he will soon come again “to judge the world with righteousness, and his people with equity.” We are to believe most firmly that this is so; for this is the testimony of God the Father, when he said, “This is My beloved Son. Listen to Him!” God the Holy Spirit testifies to this as well; for the Spirit has borne witness to Christ, both in the inspired word and by various miracles, and by his working in the hearts of men. We are to believe that this testimony is true.

Faith also believes that Christ will do what he has promised; that since he has promised to cast out none who come to him, it is certain that he will not cast #emph[us] out if we come to him. Faith believes that since Jesus said, “The water I give him will become in him a well of water springing up to eternal life,” it must be true; and if #emph[we] get this living water from Christ, it will remain in #emph[us], and will well up within #emph[us] in streams of holy life. Whatever Christ has promised to do, he will do, and we must believe this, so that we look to his hands for pardon, justification, preservation, and eternal glory, just as he has promised them to those who believe in him.

Then comes the next necessary step. Jesus is what he is said to be; Jesus will do what he says he will do; therefore each one of us must #emph[trust him], saying, “He will be to me what he says he is, and he will do for me what he has promised to do; I leave myself in the hands of the one appointed to save, so that he may save me. I rest on his promise that he will do just as he has said.” This is a saving faith, and he who has it has everlasting life. Whatever his dangers and difficulties, whatever his darkness and depression, whatever his weaknesses and sins, he who believes in Christ Jesus in this way is not condemned, and will never come into condemnation.

May that explanation be of some use! I trust the Spirit of God may use it to lead my reader into peace at once. “Do not be afraid; only believe.” Trust, and be at rest.

My fear is that the reader may be content to understand what is to be done, and yet never do it. Better the poorest real faith actually at work than the finest ideal of it left in the realm of theory. The great thing is to believe in the Lord Jesus #emph[at once]. Never mind distinctions and definitions. A hungry man eats though he does not understand the composition of his food, the anatomy of his mouth, or the process of digestion: he lives because he eats. Another, far cleverer man understands the science of nutrition thoroughly; but if he does not eat he will die, for all his knowledge. There are, no doubt, many in hell at this hour who understood the doctrine of faith, but did not believe. On the other hand, not one who has trusted in the Lord Jesus has ever been cast out, though he may never have been able to define his faith intelligently. O dear reader, receive the Lord Jesus into your soul, and you will live forever. “#smallcaps[He who believes in Him has everlasting life].”
